Build the DS core against a current toolchain.

Three unrelated breakages, all from devkitA64 now shipping GCC 15.2.0 while
the buildbot stays on something older. None is Switch-specific; any platform
building this with a recent compiler hits them.

1. A real defect the compiler finally refused:

       rsemaphore.c:104:7: error: 'return' with no value, in function
       returning non-void [-Wreturn-mismatch]

   ssem_trywait returns bool and its null check returns nothing. GCC made
   this an error by default in 14. false is the only defensible value: a
   semaphore that does not exist has not been acquired, and every caller
   reads the result as "did I get it".

2. and 3. Declarations that used to arrive transitively and no longer do,
   which is libstdc++ tightening its own includes rather than anything
   melonDS changed:

       libretro.cpp:60:10:        'time' was not declared in this scope
       Arm64Emitter.cpp:2111:35:  'numeric_limits' is not a member of 'std'

The <algorithm> additions beyond Arm64Emitter were found by checking every
source for a std symbol whose header it never includes, rather than waiting
for each to surface one build at a time. Three rounds of one-error-at-a-time
was enough. Files belonging to the desktop frontend and to the x64 JIT are
deliberately untouched: neither is in an aarch64 libretro build, and
patching them would be churn on code this never compiles.

An include added to a file that turns out not to need it costs nothing; a
missing one costs a full build cycle to discover.

